{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n71 \n \n \nas Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP). On GMT 031, Pyotr Dubrov replaced the gas bottles at CIR Manifold #4 \nto support continuation of Flame Design test points. \n \n \nFigure 1. Temperature Measurement of a Flame Design Inverse Flame \n \nIn this false-colored image, one 14-micron ceramic fiber was inserted into the flame where a second fiber \nis visible just above. Both fibers are hot enough to visibly glow, where the temperature can be \nquantitatively determined from their hue using a camera characterized for that purpose. The flame \ntemperature is determined from where the fiber passes through the flame shell and glows most brightly. \n \nWeek 17: Feb. 4-10 \nOn GMT 035, we performed the final test points for the second round of testing for ACME Flame Design. \nWe ignited 15 inverse flames. For these tests, we filled a combustion chamber with ethylene/nitrogen \nmixtures and then flowed a mixture of oxygen/nitrogen from the burner. Tests were conducted at both \nelevated and reduced pressures. The spherical symmetry of the flames, resulting from the absence of \nbuoyant flows created by Earth's gravity, simplifies data analysis and can lead to new understanding. \n \nThese were our final test points with ACME, which has been successfully performing science in CIR since \n2017! \n \n \nFigure 1. Time Sequence of the Final Flame Design Inverse Flame", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 71}}